@h1 Lists h1@

Wit has three kinds of list: a bulleted list, a numbered list, and a definition
list that pairs terms with their descriptions. Reach for one whenever content is
genuinely enumerated — steps, a works-cited list, a glossary — rather than
typing bullet characters into a paragraph, which stay literal.

@h2 Bulleted lists h2@

A bulleted list is a list node holding one item node per bullet:

@@@pre
@ul
@li Dunmore Head li@
@li Carrick Point li@
@li Fastnet Rock li@
ul@
pre@@@

Each item becomes an @code <li> code@ inside a @code <ul> code@, and the theme
draws the bullets — you never type the dash yourself.

@h2 Numbered lists h2@

Swap the list node for the ordered one and the numbers come automatically. You
do not type "1.", "2.", "3." — you write items, and the renderer numbers them:

@@@pre
@ol
@li Light the lamp at dusk. li@
@li Trim the wick every second hour. li@
@li Log the weather at dawn. li@
ol@
pre@@@

Because the numbers are generated, reordering items renumbers them for free — a
real advantage over hand-typed numbers in a draft you keep revising.

@h2 Definition lists h2@

A definition list pairs a term with its description — ideal for a glossary or a
cast of terms:

@@@pre
@dl
@dt Fresnel lens dt@
@dd A stepped lens that concentrates a lamp into a single beam. dd@
@dt Relief keeper dt@
@dd The keeper who stands in when the principal is ashore. dd@
dl@
pre@@@

The term node becomes a @code <dt> code@ and the description a @code <dd> code@,
grouped inside a @code <dl> code@.

@h2 Items hold rich content h2@

An item is inline-context: a one-line item flattens to just its text, with no
inner paragraph. But that does not limit what it can hold. An item can carry
marks, a link, or even a nested list:

@@@pre
@ul
@li The _principal_ keeper, on duty at dawn. li@
@li Supplies, delivered by tender:
@ul
@li lamp oil li@
@li spare wicks li@
ul@
li@
ul@
pre@@@

To nest a list, place a whole list node inside an item's body, as above.

@h2 A realistic works-cited list h2@

Because items take marks, a numbered list makes a clean works-cited section —
each entry italicising the work title:

@@@pre
@ol
@li Weil, Simone. _Gravity and Grace_. Routledge, 1952. li@
@li Berger, John. _Ways of Seeing_. Penguin, 1972. li@
ol@
pre@@@

For citations woven into your prose (not just a list at the end), see
@a |href /docs/write/citations/| Citations & references a@.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _Typing the bullet character yourself._ A line that begins with a dash or a
number is literal prose, not a list — Wit has no Markdown list block. Use the
list nodes and let the renderer draw the markers. li@
@li _A lone item with no list around it._ An item node on its own has no list
context. Always wrap items in a bulleted or numbered list node. li@
@li _Mismatched open and close._ Each item opens and closes with the same name;
a stray unclosed item raises an error. li@
ul@

